WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
Hosts, clients, and servers.

2
00:00:02.072 --> 00:00:05.293
MCP has three roles, and the names are precise.

3
00:00:05.573 --> 00:00:11.163
The host is the AI application the user works in: Claude Code, Claude Desktop, an IDE.

4
00:00:11.443 --> 00:00:14.024
It owns the conversation and the model calls.

5
00:00:14.304 --> 00:00:21.877
It creates and manages connections, enforces security policies and consent, and decides what context reaches the model.

6
00:00:22.157 --> 00:00:24.696
A client is a connector inside the host.

7
00:00:24.976 --> 00:00:31.077
Each client talks to exactly one server, so a host connected to five servers runs five clients.

8
00:00:31.357 --> 00:00:37.715
That one-to-one rule is a security boundary: the host, not the servers, decides what flows between them.

9
00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:44.544
A server is a program that exposes capabilities: tools to call, resources to read, prompts to offer.

10
00:00:44.824 --> 00:00:48.344
It can be a local process on your machine or a remote service.

11
00:00:48.624 --> 00:00:54.555
The specification’s design principles are worth knowing because they explain the shape of everything else.

12
00:00:54.835 --> 00:01:00.232
Servers should be extremely easy to build, with the host absorbing orchestration complexity.

13
00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:03.776
Servers should be composable, each focused on one job.

14
00:01:04.056 --> 00:01:14.104
And servers should not be able to read the whole conversation, nor see into other servers: the full history stays with the host, and each server receives only what it needs.

15
00:01:14.384 --> 00:01:25.520
When you hear “MCP gave the agent access to X,” the precise version is “the host connected a client to a server that exposes X, and the model can ask the host to call it.

16
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.098
”.

17
00:01:26.378 --> 00:01:28.703
Messages are JSON-RPC 2.

18
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:34.444
0: requests with a method name and parameters, responses with a result or an error.
